hi there Steve Kaufman here today I want  to talk about noticing in language  learning now I have said before that I  think there are three key elements in  language learning and I was quoting the  head of the language department at the  University of art was at the San Diego  State University who said that language  learning comes down to attitude time  with the language not necessarily time  in the classroom but time with the  language and the ability to notice and I  think most people understand that you  have to have a positive attitude you  have to like the language you have to  feel that you know you're confident that  you can learn the language like the  language confident of the method you're  using is good  so attitude is important time it takes  time to learn language you have to find  the time you have to you know find an  hour a day to listen as I do and so  forth but then the final one is  sometimes more difficult to explain or  describe in other words how do we  develop this ability to notice what's  happening in the language I mean we're  all aware of the sort of phenomenon that  lots of things go by us we don't notice  things and if we notice something that a  certain brand of car has a certain  feature or something else all of a  sudden we start to notice that  everywhere so that noticing something  once helps us to notice it in other  situations it's part of the sort of  acquiring of knowledge or experience and  I think it applies very much in language  learning  but and I notice myself that you know  certain things in a language that I'm  learning certain structures for a long  time or even certain elements of  pronunciation for a long time I don't  notice them then all of a sudden I start  to notice them and once I notice them in  one place say in a text that I'm reading  then I'll notice it when I listen and by  notice again in different places  eventually it starts to become part of  this new language habit that I'm  acquiring for a new language so what are  the things that help me to notice  alright  I have found that our highlighting in  link when I read on the lesson page  helps me to notice because if I see a  word that's highlighted in blue well  first of all I noticed that this is a  new word for me because I could possibly  read past that and not really notice  that this is a word that I haven't come  across before and then if I save that  word it now becomes yellow and I see the  meaning in one of the dictionaries that  I've chosen to use it linked so now it's  a yellow word but then the next time  that word shows up in a text it's now  highlighted in yellow which reminds me  that I've seen the word before because  actually in most many cases I don't even  realize that I've seen that word before  so having it highlighted helps me to  notice it similarly if I highlight a  phrase because it contains a say tense  or it contains a certain case of a noun  or an adjective I highlight it it also  forces me to notice when I see that same  phrase highlighted in yellow somewhere  else  it helps me to notice not immediately I  might then forget that but then I hear  it somewhere else or I see it again so  every time that my attention is  attracted to this word or structure it  increases the likelihood that I will  actually notice it and notice it again  and eventually learn it another thing  that I have found greatly increases my  ability to notice is listening to our  many stories because in the many stories  which I I have listened to 20 30 40  times not all at once  in other words I go lesson 1 lesson 2  lesson 3 up to lesson 10 then I go back  to lesson 1 I mean it's important to  keep these things kind of refreshing  otherwise you know the brain stops  learning it but I find that the 10th  time that I listen to this story which I  already know I know it's in the story so  I'm focusing more on the words and  phrases and I notice things I notice  words I noticed different forms of a  verb I noticed certain structures which  I didn't notice  for and so that the repetitive listening  to say a story where I'm no longer sort  of focusing on what's gonna happen in  the story but I'm focusing on the  language this helps me to notice so  another thing of course is you know I  have to want to notice I have to want to  notice I'm not just you know taking in  noise I'm actually motivated to wanna  notice words and structures in the  language I want my brain to create some  new you know patterns so that I can you  know get these new language habits so  that eventually I can use the language  so I'm motivated to acquire new habits  I'm motivated to notice the other thing  of course that helps you to notice is  the more you read and listen then there  are more and more things that you  already know so therefore just as say  with a jigsaw puzzle then the things  that you don't know stand out so you  have a better chance of noticing these  things because they stand out as being  you know things that you don't know  because all the other stuff is known if  everything is new there's a lot of stuff  that goes by you that you just don't  notice so in a way language acquisition  is a process of noticing more and more  things and as you notice them they  gradually become part of a new language  habit which you have to acquire in order  to become not only someone who  understands the language but also  someone who can speak the language so  just some thoughts on noticing as an  important part of language acquisition  thank you for listening bye for now  you
 

